d a message to... something? But that seems to be for Discord/Telegram etc.\n\nLet me think differently: perhaps as a subagent, I can just make a normal LLM call using the main agent's context? But the rules say I'm not supposed to pretend to be the main agent. However, I'm running within the same OpenClaw environment. The model is set as the default. Could I just use the standard OpenAI-compatible API? OpenRouter provides an API that's compatible with OpenAI's format. I could use `exec` to call `curl` to make an API request to OpenRouter.\n\nBut I would need an API key. The environment might have it stored. I could check if there's an environment variable like OPENROUTER_API_KEY. Let me check the environment perhaps via exec? Or I could check if there's a config file.\n\nActually, looking at the tools, there's no mention of an LLM call tool. But perhaps the main agent expects me to use the `browser` tool to access the OpenRouter web interface? Or maybe I should use `exec` to call a Python script or curl.\n\nWait, the subagent's final response should include what I accomplished. So I need to actually test the model. I should try to make an API call. Let me see if I can find any OpenRouter API key in the environment. I could try to use `exec` to print environment variables.\n\nBut before doing anything, I should check if there are any instructions files. The subagent context mentions reading certain files, but those are for main sessions. As a subagent, I'm told: \"You are NOT the main agent. Don't try to be.\" I should just do the assigned task.\n\nHow would I test the model? Perhaps the main agent expects me to use the `browser` tool to go to chat.openrouter.ai and send a message? That would be interactive and I could screenshot. But that's more complex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the `web_fetch` tool to hit the OpenRouter API endpoint directly with an API key. But I need the key.\n\nMaybe the system already has the key in an environment variable. Let me try